# Git Rules — All Agents (Strictly Enforced)

These rules apply to every agent on every machine. No exceptions.

---

## Branch Assignments

| Agent        | Branch           |
|--------------|------------------|
| orchestrator | dev (review/merge only) |
| agent-01     | agent-agent-01   |
| agent-02     | agent-agent-02   |
| agent-N      | agent-agent-N    |

---

## Rule 1 — Always Start Clean from origin/dev

Before any file is read, edited, or created:

```bash
git fetch origin
git checkout <your-branch>
git reset --hard origin/dev
```

This guarantees no stale local commits carry into the new task.

---

## Rule 2 — Commit Only Your Assigned Files

Never use `git add .` for real tasks. Always stage explicitly:

```bash
git add path/to/assigned/file1 path/to/assigned/file2
git commit -m "<type>: <short description>"
```

Commit types: feat / fix / refactor / test / config / docs / chore

Never use vague messages: fix, update, changes, WIP, misc.

---

## Rule 3 — Push Regularly

Push at minimum every 30 minutes even if a task is not complete:

```bash
git push origin <your-branch>
```

---

## Rule 4 — Never Push to main or dev Directly

Only the orchestrator merges into dev. Only the user approves merges into main.

```bash
# NEVER run these as a coder agent:
git push origin main
git push origin dev
```

---

## Rule 5 — Start of Task Checklist

```bash
# 1. Go to project root
cd /path/to/repo

# 2. Fetch latest from origin
git fetch origin

# 3. Switch to your assigned branch
git checkout <your-branch>

# 4. Reset hard to origin/dev — start clean every time
git reset --hard origin/dev

# 5. Confirm status is clean before starting
git status
```

Only proceed when `git status` shows a clean working tree.

---

## Rule 6 — End of Task Checklist

```bash
# 1. Stage ONLY your assigned files (never git add .)
git add path/to/your/assigned/file1 path/to/your/assigned/file2

# 2. Review exactly what you are committing
git status
git diff --staged

# 3. Commit with a descriptive message
git commit -m "feat: describe what was completed"

# 4. Verify diff matches ONLY your assigned files — HARD STOP if not
git diff --name-only origin/dev HEAD
# Must show ONLY files_N paths
# If ANY other file appears — DO NOT push — report scope violation to orchestrator

# 5. Check for empty commits vs dev
git log origin/dev..HEAD --oneline
# If 0 lines → nothing new → skip push and inform orchestrator

# 6. Push only if scope is clean and commits are new
git push --force-with-lease origin <your-branch>

# 7. Report completion to orchestrator
```

### Commit Subject Style

- Use only ASCII characters in the subject line — no `§`, `→`, or other symbols
- Keep subjects under 72 characters
- Format: `<type>: <short description>` (feat / fix / docs / test / chore / refactor)
- Body may contain Unicode if needed for clarity

---

## Rule 7 — Conflict Resolution

If you see CONFLICT during any git operation — STOP immediately.
Do not touch the file. Report to the orchestrator immediately:

```
CONFLICT DETECTED
Agent: <your-agent-name>
Branch: <your-branch>
File: <filename>
Awaiting instruction from orchestrator.
```

---

## Rule 8 — Do Not Commit These Files

Make sure `.gitignore` covers these before committing:

```
node_modules/
.env
.env.*
.agent-identity
*.log
dist/
build/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.sqlite
*.key
*.pem
.venv/
__pycache__/
```

---

## Rule 9 — .agent-identity Is Local

`.agent-identity` is gitignored and machine-local. Never commit it, never
force-add it, never copy secret values (Discord webhook URLs, etc.) into
tracked files.

---

## Rule 10 — Force-Push Policy

Force-push is permitted on agent branches only under these conditions:

| Situation | Command | Who |
|-----------|---------|-----|
| Pre-task reset to origin/dev (automatic) | `git push --force-with-lease origin <branch>` | run-agent-task.sh |
| Orchestrator-requested branch cleanup | `git push --force-with-lease origin <branch>` | orchestrator only |
| Any other reason | NOT permitted — report to orchestrator first | — |

Always use `--force-with-lease` — never `--force`.
Never force-push to dev or main.

---

## Rule 11 — Git Identity

Every agent must have its git identity set to its agent name and project email:

```bash
git config --global user.name "agent-01"
git config --global user.email "agent-01@your-domain.com"
```

Verify identity before starting any task:

```bash
git config user.name
git config user.email
```

Remove local repo overrides if present:

```bash
git config --local --unset user.name 2>/dev/null || true
git config --local --unset user.email 2>/dev/null || true
```

---

## Rule 12 — One Clean Commit Per Slice

Each dispatch slice should result in exactly **one commit** on the agent branch
containing only the declared `files_N` paths. Before pushing:

```bash
# 1. Check what you have vs dev
git log origin/dev..HEAD --oneline
# If this shows commits already on dev → squash or drop them:
git rebase -i origin/dev
# Mark already-merged commits as 'drop'

# 2. Verify final diff matches only your assigned files
git diff --name-only origin/dev HEAD
# Must match files_N exactly

# 3. Only push if there is new content
# 0 lines = nothing new = do not push
git log origin/dev..HEAD --oneline | wc -l
```

The `run-agent-task.sh` script enforces this automatically — it checks for
new commits vs dev before pushing and skips the push entirely if there is
nothing new, posting an informational Discord message instead.

---

## Quick Reference Card

| Situation | Command |
|-----------|---------|
| Start of task | `git fetch origin && git checkout <branch> && git reset --hard origin/dev` |
| Stage assigned files only | `git add path/to/file1 path/to/file2` |
| Review staged changes | `git diff --staged` |
| Commit | `git commit -m "type: description"` |
| Push | `git push origin <branch>` |
| Force-push after reset | `git push --force-with-lease origin <branch>` |
| Conflict detected | **STOP — report to orchestrator** |
| Verify git identity | `git config user.name && git config user.email` |

---

*These rules are non-negotiable. Every agent follows them on every task, every time.*
